      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Therefore light’s speed can be expressed as 1 light‑year per year.",
      "evidence": "If light covers one light‑year in one year, speed = distance/time = 1 ly / 1 yr = 1 ly·yr⁻¹.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "The travel time (in years) for light to cover a distance measured in light‑years equals that distance numerically.",
      "evidence": "Time = Distance ÷ Speed; using Distance = N ly and Speed = 1 ly/yr gives Time = N years.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "For a star 125,000 light‑years away, the light has been traveling for 125,000 years.",
      "evidence": "Apply step 3 with N = 125,000: Time = 125,000 years.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Option (C) \"125,000 years\" matches the calculated travel time.",
      "evidence": "The answer list includes (C) as 125,000 years, which equals the result from step 4.",
